==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|gunþiz|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|gʷʰéntis||killing, blow|lang=ine-pro}}, from {{recons|gʷʰen-||hit, drive|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Saxon ''[[gudea|gūdea]]'', Old High German ''gund-'' (in ''[[gundfano]]'' ‘war-flag’), Old Norse ''[[guðr]], [[gunnr]]''. The Indo-European root is also the source of Latin ''[[defendo|defendere]]'' and ''[[offendo|offendere]]'', Slavic ''*gъnati-'' (Old Church Slavonic [[гънати]], Russian [[гнать]]), Avestan {{term|𐬘𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬙𐬌|sc=Avst||to kill|tr=jainti}}, Sanskrit {{term|हन्ति|tr=hanti||to slay|lang=sa}}, Baltic ''*giñ-'' (Lithuanian ''[[giñti]]'') as well as Old Irish "[[gonaid]]".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɡuːθ/|lang=ang}}

